<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sidonius Apollinaris</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 430–c. 485</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sidonius Apollinaris was a Gallo-Roman aristocrat, poet and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Clermont from about 470. Son-in-law of the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avitus">Avitus</a>, he wrote panegyrics for three emperors and a collection of letters that are a major source for fifth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>. He led Clermont's resistance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> until it was ceded to them in 475, and he described <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> as the most ferocious of enemies.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sidonius-apollinaris.jpg" alt="Sidonius Apollinaris" width="320" height="297" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SidonClermont.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gaius Sollius Modestus Apollinaris Sidonius was born at Lugdunum, the modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a>, into the Gallo-Roman aristocracy. His father was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prefect">Prefect</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valentinian-iii">Valentinian III</a>, and his grandfather Apollinaris held the same office from 408 to 409. Around 452 he married Papianilla, the daughter of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avitus">Avitus</a>.</p><p>His father-in-law became emperor in 455, and Sidonius wrote a panegyric for him. When Majorian deprived <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avitus">Avitus</a> of the empire in 457 and seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyons</a>, Sidonius fell into his hands, but his reputation for learning earned him respectful treatment. He composed a panegyric for Majorian, which won him a statue at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and the title of comes. In 468 the emperor Anthemius appointed him Urban Prefect of Rome, probably in part to win the support of the aristocrats of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, and later made him a Patrician and Senator.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Sidonius was elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Clermont in succession to Eparchius, a relative of his wife. He says little about it and seems not to have wanted the post. He declared that he would give up pagan poetry as incompatible with religious life, but he went on writing verse privately. From 473 to 475 he and his brother-in-law Ecdicus led the defence of Clermont, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euric">Euric</a> attacked every year.</p><p>When the city was finally conquered he was imprisoned and exiled to Liviana, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euric">Euric</a> allowed him to return as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> in 476 or 477 after the intervention of his adviser Leo. Sidonius accepted a degree of collaboration with the Visigothic court as necessary to keep the Roman aristocracy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> together, yet he remained hostile to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>. He wrote to a senator, You shun barbarians because they have a bad reputation; I avoid them, even when they have a good one.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Twenty-four Carmina survive, occasional verse including panegyrics on emperors that draw on Statius, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ausonius">Ausonius</a> and Claudian. Nine books of Letters preserve 147 documents addressed to 117 people, published in part in the early 470s and gathered in their final form around 477. Eric Goldberg calls him the single most important surviving author from 5th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>.</p><p>His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> is deliberately elaborate and dense with classical and biblical allusion, which Sigrid Mratschek sees as a bulwark against the barbarians and a refuge from the dissolution of the old order. His letters cannot be read as plain description, since he modelled them on earlier letter writers, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-younger">Pliny the Younger</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sidonius Apollinaris” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The poet <b>Sidonius Apollinaris</b>, writing around 480, describes Saxon pirates as masters of the sea who drowned one in ten of their captives as a sacrifice before sailing home.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saxons">Saxons · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Lugdunum, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Arvernis. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.76,4.82&amp;year=465" aria-label="Lugdunum on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4664 551 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Sidonius Apollinaris"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5114 821h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5114 821h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.76,4.82&amp;year=465">What was here in 465 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
